cursynth - a GNU program
------------
cursynth is a polyphonic, MIDI enabled, subtractive synth that runs in your
terminal with an ascii interface.

cursynth will connect to all MIDI devices and send output to the default audio
device on your system.

### Building

    $ autoreconf -i
    $ ./configure
    $ make
    $ sudo make install        # optional

What you need first, by name, because "it will not build" was the most common
thing anybody ever reported about cursynth and the answer was always a package
nobody had mentioned:

  Debian, Ubuntu:
    sudo apt-get install build-essential automake autoconf autopoint \
                         pkg-config gettext libncurses-dev libasound2-dev

  macOS, with Homebrew:
    brew install automake autoconf pkg-config gettext

  Windows, in an MSYS2 MINGW64 shell:
    pacman -S base-devel autoconf automake gettext-devel \
              mingw-w64-x86_64-gcc mingw-w64-x86_64-pkgconf \
              mingw-w64-x86_64-ncurses mingw-w64-x86_64-gettext

Two more are optional and the build says so rather than stopping:

  lv2-dev   (Debian, Ubuntu) or  brew install lv2      builds the LV2 plugin
  texinfo                                              builds the manual

gettext does not have to be on the default include path. configure asks
Homebrew where it is and looks in the usual places, and takes
--with-gettext-prefix=DIR if it still cannot find it. If you have no gettext
at all, ./configure --disable-nls builds a cursynth that speaks English.

    $ make check

runs the tests: the oscillator measured by its spectrum and by its shape, and
the LV2 plugin loaded, played and checked that it answers its controls.

### Usage
cursynth [--buffer-size OR -b preferred-buffer-size]
         [--sample-rate OR -s preferred-sample-rate]
         [--version OR -V]

### Controls
* awsedftgyhujkolp;' - a playable keyboard (no key up events)
* \`1234567890 - a slider for the current selected control
* up/down - previous/next control
* left/right - decrement/increment control
* F1 (or [shift] + H) - help/controls
* [shift] + L - browse/load patches
* [shift] + S - save patch
* m - arm midi learn
* c - erase midi learn

### Requirements:
* OS: Mac OSX or GNU/Linux
* Terminal: a color enabled terminal, at least 120x44 characters. Anything
  smaller is refused with a message saying so, rather than drawn half off the
  screen. Making the display work in less room is still to do.
* If you want key up events, you have to use a MIDI keyboard

